# A local Android build runs Gradle, and Gradle rewrites library manifests in
# place inside node_modules (it strips the `package` attribute). The iOS
# fingerprint hashes whole package directories for autolinking, so one Android
# build was enough to stop the next iOS build matching. A library's manifest
# only changes with its version or a patch, and both are hashed elsewhere.
# See TODO-ANDROID.md.
node_modules/**/android/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml

# react-native-executorch's postinstall writes into its own package directory:
# prebuilt libraries and headers under third-party/, and rne-build-config.json,
# which records the backends and libs chosen in our package.json. None of that
# tracks the package version, so a checkout's copy drifts from a fresh install:
#
# - the iOS frameworks only download on macOS, so a Mac checkout never matched
#   an Android build made on Linux;
# - the extractor only adds files, so dropping a backend leaves its libraries
#   behind until node_modules is deleted;
# - pnpm does not rerun a postinstall for a package it already has, so changing
#   the block in package.json leaves the old rne-build-config.json in place.
#
# All three end as "Runtime version mismatch". The outputs are ignored here and
# their inputs are hashed instead: `nativeLibsVersion` in the package's own
# package.json, which is still hashed, and our package.json block, which
# fingerprint.config.js adds. See TODO-ANDROID.md.
node_modules/react-native-executorch/third-party/ios/**
node_modules/react-native-executorch/third-party/android/libs/**
node_modules/react-native-executorch/third-party/include/**
node_modules/react-native-executorch/rne-build-config.json
